BOUNDARY: The quantitative estimate was tested against the definitions of mesh and grid partition. For each interior hyperplane of \(P\), the affected cells of a mesh-\(\delta\) grid indeed lie within a slab of thickness \(\le 2\delta\); their total volume is bounded by \(2\delta\prod_{r\neq j}(b_r-a_r)\). Summing over the finitely many interior hyperplanes gives a constant \(C_P\) (e.g., \(C_P=2\sum\prod_{r\neq j}(b_r-a_r)\)) such that the change in either Darboux sum is \(\le 2B C_P\delta\). No counterexample was found; the bound holds uniformly for all bounded \(f\) with \(|f|\le B\) and all grids of mesh \(\delta\). The constant depends only on \(P\) and the rectangle, not on \(\delta\) or the particular fine grid. The first part (monotonicity under refinement) is standard and correctly argued by induction on inserted hyperplanes.

